      "id": "yntyh6z",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Photosynthesis"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7ait7qv",
      "content": "Photosynthesis is the process by which plants prepare food using sunlight, carbon dioxide and water."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1joukcu",
      "content": "Equation: 6CO2 + 6H2O → C6H12O6 + 6O2"
    },
    {
      "id": "djh5mla",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Types of Plant Nutrition"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Xylem",
        "Phloem"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Main Function",
          "Transport of water and mineral salts",
          "Transport of prepared food (sucrose)"
        ],
        [
          "Direction of Flow",
          "Unidirectional (upwards from roots)",
          "Bidirectional (from leaves to other parts)"
        ],
        [
          "Nature of Cells",
          "Mostly dead elements (Tracheids, Vessels)",
          "Mostly living elements (Sieve tubes, Companion cells)"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "83j97md"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r7skbpm",
      "content": "• Autotrophic nutrition – plants make their own food."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4g8rdps",
      "content": "• Parasitic plants – obtain nutrients from host (e.g., Cuscuta)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2q0zfcw",
      "content": "• Insectivorous plants – trap insects for nutrients (e.g., pitcher plant)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3p678nx",
      "content": "• Symbiotic plants – mutual relationship with other organisms (e.g., lichens)."
    },
